"Taiwan is a small island, yet people all over the world call it a chip island. Phones, computers, cars, and the servers that run AI depend on chips that passed through Taiwan. How did this happen? It did not happen by luck, and it did not happen quickly. It took about fifty years, and it began with a plan. In 1973 the government founded a research institute called ITRI in Hsinchu. A year later, a small group of officials and engineers met to discuss how Taiwan could start making integrated circuits. In 1976 teams of young engineers were sent to RCA, a company in the United States, to learn how. They came home and built a demonstration factory, and it worked well. The next step was to turn research into companies. In 1980 UMC was spun off from ITRI and became Taiwan's first chip company. In the same year Hsinchu Science Park opened beside two universities, so that companies, engineers, and students could be close together. Today hundreds of companies have their homes in that park. Then came the big idea. In those days, a chip company did everything itself: it designed its chips and also built the expensive factories to make them. In 1987 Morris Chang founded TSMC to do only one part. It would manufacture chips for any company that had a design, and it would not design chips of its own. This kind of company is called a foundry. The foundry changed the industry. Now a small team with a good idea could design a chip without building a factory. Design companies, called fabless companies, grew up around the world. And Taiwan filled in the other steps as well: design companies such as MediaTek in Hsinchu, and packaging and testing companies such as ASE in Kaohsiung. So a chip's trip can look like this: designed in one place, manufactured in a science park, packaged and tested in another city, and then shipped to the world. Hsinchu, Taichung, Tainan, and Kaohsiung all lie along the west coast of one small island, and that closeness helps. The chip island was built by many people over many years, one step at a time.": "64b68644656e",
